(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2809052250-effect-consistency-classification-draft")
  (:adr/title "Effect consistency classification as a compiler-manifest property (draft vocabulary, not compiler-landed)")
  (:adr/status "proposed")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-05")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2607279200" "ADR-2809051130"))
  (:adr/context
  "The accepted design direction (2026-09-04/05 sessions) is that consistency is a property of an effect, not of a database or a deployment: every admitted effect carries one of :local / :validated / :countersigned / :inga-qc n, VM selection is orthogonal, transitions are validated by peer re-execution (computed root == declared root), and fork detection produces signed evidence rather than global canonicalization. Surveyed this tick, all reads from kotoba-lang origin/main 45c7d0fccb30c70437225bf47016e9f8a9508497 in worktree kototama-consistency-3-lang: (1) lang/capability-semantics.edn v2 already requires compiler facts to be bound to world CIDs - ':compiler-facts :must-be-bound-to-definition-kir-effects-semantics-and-world-cids' under :kotoba.logic-manifest/v1 :logic, and names \"amu:\" facts :verified-compiler-manifest. (2) grep ':consistency' across the whole kotoba-lang tree returns nothing - the vocabulary does not exist anywhere yet; lang/surface-status.edn (profile 6, 2026-08-30) has a :dispositions axis (safety/simplification) that is orthogonal to consistency and must not absorb it. (3) docs/lang/distributed-authority-threat-model-2026-08-15.edn scopes :inga-total-order with threat T05 (fake-consensus-certificate) mitigated by canonical-inga-block-hash / admitted-validator-set / distinct-witness-quorum / per-vote-signature, and its :replication-to-order boundary already states 'set union remains replication; only a bound Inga QC mints ordered commit' - this is exactly the seam where a :countersigned claim must stop and an :inga-qc claim must begin. (4) lang/code-identity.edn records :package-manifest-cid :status :not-implemented, so a manifest-level :consistency property has no natural landing surface in code today; writing it into the compiler now would be an unverifiable claim. Per ADR-2607279200, language-surface changes land only with a passing compile; this ADR therefore proposes the vocabulary and its binding rules, and the compiler change stays open until a compile exercises it.")
  (:adr/decision
  "1. Vocabulary: four consistency classes, statically attached per effect in the compiler manifest, never inferred from runtime behavior. :local - the effect's result is authoritative for the acting host alone; no external validation is claimed. :validated - at least one peer has re-executed the transition and confirmed computed root == declared root; the claim is only as strong as the named peer set recorded alongside it. :countersigned - a named authority signature covers the transition, subject to the threat model's admission boundaries (data never reconstructs authority). :inga-qc n - the transition is mints-ordered by an Inga quorum certificate of depth n; only this class may claim total order, per the :replication-to-order boundary rule. Classes are ordered for admission checks but the order is monotone in evidence, not in safety: :inga-qc 1 does not subsume :countersigned from a different authority. 2. Binding: the manifest must bind each :consistency value to the same identity keys capability-semantics already mandates - definition KIR, effects, semantics, and world CIDs - so a consistency claim is content-addressed and cannot be replayed against a different world. Claims whose world CID cannot be resolved are :local by fail-closed default. 3. Orthogonality: :consistency is independent of the VM/target keyword (ADR-2809051130 naming) and independent of surface :dispositions; a surface-status entry never sets or widens consistency. 4. Landing path: (a) vocabulary + binding rules in capability-semantics.edn :logic (new :consistency-classes map) and a surface-status invariant entry declaring no-implicit-consistency-promotion (a :local effect never becomes :validated by transport, caching, or replication); (b) compiler manifest emission only after a kotoba compile run actually admits and rejects per the vocabulary; (c) fork evidence vocabulary (task 4) reuses this classification for what a peer signs when it detects double history.")
  (:adr/consequences
  "Nothing in any compiler or runtime changes under this ADR alone; it creates the shared vocabulary and records the fail-closed default (:local) so no consumer can claim more consistency than its evidence class. Consumers that would have invented ad-hoc consistency language now have one place to bind to. Follow-ups, each gated on its own passing verification: capability-semantics :consistency-classes entry (needs a repo-side edit + conformance note), compiler manifest emission (needs compile evidence), peer re-execution validation protocol and signed fork-evidence format (task 4 survey). The draft explicitly does NOT claim that any current amu/kototama EVM artifact carries any class above :local - per the implementation declaration (kototama qualification/implementation-declaration-amu-evm.edn, :status :partial, :levels [:bytecode]) no conformance evidence exists for state-level claims.")))
